        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "I notice an existing sample but I'm sticking to the brief and not opening other samples, relying on docs instead since no africanElephantSonnet.ts exists yet. I'm starting to sketch out the design—an African bush elephant with realistic proportions like shoulder height around 3.2 meters.\n\nNow I'm working out the skeleton and measurements in detail: body barrel dimensions, shoulder and hip heights accounting for the characteristic dipped back, head and skull positioning, and trunk length and curvature hanging down to near ground level in a relaxed resting pose, using low-poly faceted shading for the geometric style.\n\nFor the legs, I'm planning them as nearly-straight columnar pillars with front shoulder and foot joint positions defined, using a multi-segment limb chain to get a slight natural bend rather than a stiff single cylinder.\n\nDefining leg path points...\n\nDefining more joint chains...\n\nFor sides I'll go with 8-10 and flat shading so the facets read clearly as low-poly. I want to avoid flat untextured colors reading as unfinished, so I'll add surface variation through mottling and grain, plus a dusty gradient toward the lower legs to suggest dirt and earth tones—paint carrying the texture rather than relying on geometry alone.\n\nI'm settling on a restrained palette—greys, pink ear interior, ivory tusks, dark mud ochre—while keeping the low-poly flat-shaded facets crisp. Now I'm sketching the skeleton hierarchy: root hips, spine chain, neck, head, and a separate jaw joint positioned below the trunk base.\n\nI'm continuing to plan the trunk as a tentacle-role chain of five joints, ears as hinge chains with two joints per flap shaped like Africa's silhouette, rigid tusks and tail nails, and leg/tail chains using standard roles—with the ear geometry extruded along its own plane with a slight outward tilt.\n\nDefining ear attachment angles and outline coordinates...\n\nNow I'm planning the ear's paint pattern with pink-grey veins and darker wrinkled rims, then setting up the joint rig—a hinge joint at the attachment point with a tip joint further out to allow flexing, considering whether a simple rigid extrude or a multi-joint chain works better for bending.\n\nFor the head, I'm thinking through how to build the elephant's skull shape—a domed forehead and cheeks—deciding between a loft along a chain of stations or simpler scaled ellipsoid spheres for the cranium and trunk root.\n\nContinuing down toward the trunk root and narrowing the final skull station near the face, then setting up the trunk root radius and figuring out where the head center and neck joint chain should sit relative to the chest so the skull overlaps the shoulders naturally without looking detached.\n\nNow for the trunk, I'm sketching a chain from the face root curling forward and downward to a tapered tip, around 7 joints with radius shrinking from 0.28 to 0.09, parented to the head as a tentacle-role bone, keeping the sweep sides at 8 for a slightly faceted look.\n\nFor surface detail, I'm painting wrinkle rings along the trunk using sharp sine-based dark stripe bands plus a lighter pinkish underside sector, then adding two small finger-like spikes at the tip, dark nostril disc shapes on the end cap, and orienting the tip tangent forward-up.\n\nChecking the trunk tip position relative to the floor confirms it hangs clear without touching, so I'm moving on to building the lower jaw as a small frustum wedge below the trunk root, giving it a darker lip line and a pinkish inner mouth patch for the characteristic rounded elephant lip.\n\nNow I'm plotting the tusks as curved ivory cones sprouting from the upper jaw sides, using a bezier path that arcs outward, down, forward, then curves back up and inward to mimic a mature bull's ~1.5-1.8m exposed tusks, with a tapered radius, pointed cap, and a darker sheath ring at the base where it meets the lip.\n\nFor the eyes, I'm placing small spheres on the sides of the head with black irises, a brow ridge, tiny white highlights, and a few thin lash spikes, plus a subtle dark temporal gland streak below the eye. Now I'm setting up the tail as a chain of segments hanging down from the rump point.\n\nSince elephants have sparse hair, I'll add a small tuft on the head crown using dark bristle cards, plus knee wrinkles as flat ring sweeps at leg joints. I'm also adding body fold lines—thin dark tube sweeps at the shoulder behind the ear, neck, and thigh crease—using about six low-radius loop sweeps for the crease effect.\n\nNow I'm working out the feet: toenails as small cream wedge boxes (4 front, 3-4 hind), a bulky foot pad cylinder with a cuticle bump ring sunk slightly below the leg bottom, plus some dust paint on the lower legs and ear edges for wear. I'm also adding shoulder and hip muscle bulges using flattened ellipsoid spheres positioned at the body sides for the scapula and thigh.\n\nI'm continuing with anatomical bumps -- attaching hind thigh bumps to the nearest bone, shaping a hanging belly, giving the back and rump a lumpy spine ridge with visible pelvis/hip bumps as small raised ellipsoids, adding a tail root bump, shaping the forehead into twin domes with a temple dimple, thickening the ear root attachment folds, adding a dark crease at the mouth corner, a brow ridge, chin wrinkles via ring paint with lighter underside shading, and a small neck/dewlap skin fold.\n\nFor body hair, I'll sparse bristles across the lumpy body but skip most of it except a head tuft and chin. For mud, I'm giving the lower legs an earthy African red-dust tint with a gradient, and considering dusting the back and bottom with reddish-tan ochre mixed into the grey mottling based on height. For wrinkles, I'm designing a paint function that works by position and sweep angle -- horizontal ring creases around the body depending on sweep orientation, and ring creases at the knee and ankle regions on the legs.\n\nDesigning a generic wrinkle function that uses noise-warped position to create crease patterns, applying it differently per body part: ring-like bands along the legs and trunk using vertical coordinates, and creases wrapping a different axis on the body. I'm also thinking about how densely to paint each part given the overall model's texel budget.\n\nWorking through texel density: with ~25m² surface area, a 2048px paint resolution gives roughly 3mm per texel, which should be sharp enough to capture 1-2cm creases without excessive cost compared to 4096. I'm settling on 2048 as the right balance between crispness and a low-poly aesthetic.\n\nFor the elephant's skin, I realize Worley cell noise (taking the difference between nearest and second-nearest distances to get thin lines) naturally produces the cracked, polygonal crackle pattern real elephant skin has, so I'm switching to that approach with small cell sizes for the wrinkle network.\n\nI'm layering darker tone along cell borders where the distance difference is small, varying each cell's base tone slightly by its ID, and adding coarser ring creases on top for the classic cracked-skin look. Shading itself I'll leave flat, letting the renderer's lighting handle depth.\n\nCounting joints: spine, hips, neck, head, jaw, trunk segments, ears, tail, and four legs each with several joints brings the total to around 38, which feels reasonable without needing separate toe-digit joints beyond the leg tips.\n\nEstimating total part count, factoring in toenails, eyes, lashes, and skin creases, I land somewhere around 150-250 parts, consistent with the level of detail expected for markings, fur, claws, teeth, eye detail, and accessories -- I'm also considering adding tusk growth rings as ridges for extra realism.\n\nNow I'm adding more surface details: tusk cracks, a bit of inner mouth pink, eyelids and lashes, dark temporal gland streaks on the cheeks, painted ear veins and holes, torn notches along the ear edges, mud splatter patches on the flanks, scar scratches, bristly forehead hair, chin whiskers, a tail tuft, sparse back hair cards, toenail variation, foot wrinkles, worn scratches near the tusk tips with a stained dark tip, and a tusk base sheath. I'm also planning a small trunk fingertip, nostril detail, and transverse ridge rolls along the trunk's underside to mimic real elephant skin folds.\n\nFor the trunk ridges, I'm working out whether to oscillate the trunk radius along its length using a wave function so ridges appear in silhouette without needing a heavy geometric sweep -- balancing ridge spacing against vertex count to keep the low-poly style manageable, aiming for roughly 20 ridges with flat-shaded faceting. I'm deciding to keep the body at 10 sides with flat shading for that faceted look, and considering a loft-based approach for the body to get a more distinct belly silhouette.\n\nI'm sketching out station parameters (position, center height, width, height) for the body loft, thinking through the rump cap shape and whether the loft naturally supports rounded end caps -- leaning toward using a sweep with a radius function instead since it guarantees proper rounded caps more reliably.\n\nNow I'm mapping out a spine curve as a sequence of catmull points from tail to shoulders, with a radius function controlling cross-section width/height and a shift parameter for belly sag, then figuring out how many joints this spine needs if parented from the hips.\n\nPelvis sits at P1 (z=-0.9), and I'm splitting the body path into a front chain running forward from hips to shoulders and a rear chain running backward from hips toward the tail base, keeping both chains rooted at the same hip joint so the body bends naturally from one pivot.\n\nGood, so root joint role should be \"spine\". Now I'm working out the radii profile along the body path as a function of t, defining rx/ry half-extents at key points (rear cap, barrel, etc.) using real elephant proportions as a reference, with smooth interpolation between keyframes.\n\nI'm also adding a shoulder/withers bulge ellipsoid and working out how the front leg chain attaches inside the body so the transition between leg and torso blends smoothly.\n\nFor leg thickness, I'm thinking through elephant proportions - using the rule that foot circumference roughly equals half the shoulder height, so legs need to be columnar and thick, tapering slightly from a wide upper section down through the elbow and ankle to the foot, with a diameter around 0.5m at the base.\n\nI'm also working out the hind leg taper (thigh to knee to ankle) and sketching the joint positions for the front leg chain, using x-coordinates mirrored by side (left vs right) with slight angles between shoulder, elbow, wrist, and foot joints.\n\nAdding foot and nail geometry...\n\nNow I'm plotting the hind leg joint chain with knee and ankle points, then adding knee cap bumps for the hind legs. I'm also sketching overall body proportions from tail to front, keeping the rest pose with straight legs, hanging trunk, outstretched ears, and closed jaw in mind.\n\nI'm setting up the head joint position and orientation, noting the neck is short so the head loft overlaps the front of the body sweep, which should blend naturally. I'm deciding to define the head as a loft with stations rather than a separate sweep, starting to lay out the station coordinates.\n\nStill writing loft stations... I should check the loft function's signature in the builder source to confirm what options like sides are supported before finalizing the call.\n\n",
